-> Logging :
    - we write 'logs' to know the flow of real time data.
    - Logger prints logs into 'files' and can also into 'console' too.
    - LOGS are used to debug later i.e. to know what happened actually, we check Logs
    - Two major Java related Logging Frameworks (vendors) :
        1. 'LOG4j' - oldest
            - the newer version of LOG4j is 'LOG4j2' - modern
        2. 'Logback' - new
    - SpringBoot by default uses Logback
    - Instead of directly using a Framework / Implementation, we use Specification called - 'Slf4j'
    - 'Slf4j' - can use underline implementation like,
                                        Logback, LOG4j, LOG4j2

    - Logging Levels :
        TRACE - All raw info
        DEBUG
        INFO
        WARN
        ERROR - exception
        FATAL
    (let say, if we turn on LEVEL -> INFO, only INFO, WARN, ERROR are logged. TRACE and DEBUG wont gets logged. same way for all...)
    - If we want to change the level of logging, we need to add this line in application.properties,
                logging.level.org.springframework=DEBUG / logging.level.root=DEBUG  // instead of root, we can also mention the base package what we need
        - SpringBoot by default on's INFO.

    - To write Logs, we need Logger Object. for that we need LoggerFactory which gives Logger.
            private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ClassName.class);
        - Now inside methods, we can write logs,
            logger.trace("Request received for add item in Item Controller");
            logger.debug("Received ItemRequestDTO , {}", addItemRequestDTO);  // using placeholder
    - By adding, logging.file.name=app.log , logs can be sent to files ; where 'app' is the file name and '.log' is extension.

